00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.480
is take some of the common
questions, common themes,

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:37.610
and answer them here for the
benefit of the larger group.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.920
So let's start with the
cloud adoption patterns.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.110
So I want to talk about
two things really.

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:46.970
The first thing is the cloud
is a well-understood thing,

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.550
with huge economic
and business benefits,

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.520
agility benefits,
productivity benefits.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:53.990
People want to move to Cloud.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:56.870
Enterprises and customers
are willing to move to cloud.

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.261
But there is some hesitation.

00:01:58.261 --> 00:02:00.260
As they move their workloads
from their existing

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:05.060
infrastructure to cloud or from
one cloud to another cloud.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:08.990
The reason is because most of
the core systems that they have

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:12.050
are hardwired to the
UIs, are hardwired

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.265
to the client or the experiences
that they're building.

00:02:15.265 --> 00:02:16.640
So the moment you
think of moving

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.760
one workload from an
existing infrastructure

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.519
and putting it in
the cloud platform,

00:02:21.519 --> 00:02:23.060
you're worried about
what will break.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:24.890
You don't know which
of the integrations

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.720
are going to break,
which of the clients

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.770
will complain that
they're having issues.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:32.180
So one of the common themes
we're seeing with API platform

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:35.360
as the abstraction layer,
once you have API management

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.790
platform as a layer through
which all your clients are

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.310
coming into your workloads--
whether they are on-prem

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:41.450
or on the cloud--

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.830
you have flexibility
to move things around.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.200
You can know who is impacted.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.280
You can know what has to be
done to rectify those impacts.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.010
And you have the flexibility
to move your workloads from,

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:56.082
let's say, an existing backend
to a cloud-based pattern.

00:02:56.082 --> 00:02:57.790
That is one common
pattern we are seeing.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:58.940
It's a very simple pattern.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.190
Once you have API management
as an abstraction layer,

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:04.190
you can move things around.

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:05.640
That's one pattern we see.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:09.680
The second pattern we are
seeing is IT modernization.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.260
As enterprises adopt
app modernization or IT

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.690
modernization, what we're
seeing is containers

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:16.280
are becoming a huge driver.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:18.050
The reason is really simple.

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:21.530
Enterprises are looking to build
what we call as hybrid clouds.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:23.300
A hybrid cloud
really is a scenario

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:26.960
where you have your applications
running both on-prem

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.490
and on the cloud.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:30.710
And you want to have
a seamless experience

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.870
across these applications
that are running on-prem

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:34.090
as well as on the cloud.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:36.970
That's the whole premise
of where containers

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:38.990
are becoming super important.

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:43.190
So what's really happening
is, as a developer,

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.575
you write your app, package
that into your container.

00:03:45.575 --> 00:03:48.200
And the container orchestration
engine or the engine below that

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.666
is taking care of all
the abstraction for you,

00:03:50.666 --> 00:03:52.040
of the infrastructure
underneath.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.810
It's taking care
of all the scaling.

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:55.610
It's taking care
of how the VMs are

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:57.525
managed, so on and so forth.

00:03:57.525 --> 00:04:00.025
That allows you, as a developer,
to package your application

00:04:00.025 --> 00:04:03.150
into a container and just
worry about that piece of code.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.750
In a sense, at the enterprise
scale, what's happening is

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.880
you have a huge variety
of applications.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:08.936
Think of it as polyglot.

00:04:08.936 --> 00:04:11.060
There are some teams starting
applications in Java.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:13.230
Some teams starting
applications in Node.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:14.960
Some teams starting
applications in Go.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:17.240
So you have a diverse
variety of applications.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:20.260
And as an app developer,
all that you care about

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:23.290
is packaging your
application into a container

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:24.790
and give it to
orchestration engine.

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:26.373
And the orchestration
engine is taking

00:04:26.373 --> 00:04:29.060
care of the abstraction of
the infrastructure underneath.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.300
It's taking care of the
workload distribution.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.640
It's taking care
of auto scaling.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.220
It's taking care of
[INAUDIBLE] so on and so forth.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:40.550
In the same way,
what happens now

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:43.280
is once you have this diverse
variety of applications,

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:45.522
at the consumption
level, you do have

00:04:45.522 --> 00:04:47.230
to make sure that
these applications talk

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:48.120
to each other.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.869
And that's where the
API management really

00:04:49.869 --> 00:04:50.870
comes into picture.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:53.670
An app developer, again, you
are responsible for the contract

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:56.020
or what your app is doing.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:59.090
You make sure that the
APIs that you adhered to,

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:02.660
that you have the
application remain the same.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:04.300
And once they do,
you onboard them

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.600
to an API management platform.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.016
And the API
management platform is

00:05:07.016 --> 00:05:09.260
taking care of things
like security, things

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:13.760
like visibility,
routing, and making

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.020
these APIs application-specific,
allowing your business

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.040
to institute more partnerships.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.550
So in a sense, the Kubernetes
container orchestration

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:26.880
at the orchestration level,
as well as the API management

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:30.420
at the API abstraction
level, they

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:33.950
are the key drivers are to how
our enterprise customers are

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:36.170
modernizing their
IT infrastructure

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:40.470
or getting platform
modernization.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:44.500
There is a small
extension to this really.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:47.880
To start with, Greenfield
is not always a possibility.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.990
You always have
existing applications.

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:54.300
So you cannot start Greenfield
and start thinking that I'll do

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.210
containers from next month.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:02.980
Neither is it practical
for enterprises to say,

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:05.150
I'll take all my
existing infrastructure

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:06.560
and modernize it at one go.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.090
I'll take one year.

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:13.310
And starting next year, I'll my
infrastructure is modernized.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:15.110
In those cases,
again, API management

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:17.690
becomes an abstraction
layer at the higher level.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:20.590
So you do have API management
taking care of the APIs

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:22.430
that are coming from your ESBs.

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:26.000
They are coming from your
VM-based applications, as well

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.460
as the applications that
are containerized that are

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.210
in the cloud platforms.

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:39.200
So this is a practicality
that we're seeing

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:40.450
with our enterprise customers.

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:42.430
And once you have
that abstraction layer

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:46.060
as API management layer,
you do have the flexibility.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:47.977
And you can decide which
of these applications

00:06:47.977 --> 00:06:50.435
you do want to modernize and
which are the applications you

00:06:50.435 --> 00:06:51.550
want to move to the cloud.

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:55.230
And for the clients, all of
this is kind of transparent.

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:58.870
They don't have to know the
internal details of how your IP

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:01.120
infrastructure is built up.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.790
So the common theme
across these two patterns

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:07.480
really is, as long as you're
able to come to a state

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.400
where your workloads
are either consuming

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:12.010
other workloads as
APIs, or your workload

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:14.170
is being consumed
by others as APIs,

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:16.580
you have immense flexibility
to move things around.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:19.300
And that's what we're seeing
with our enterprise customer.

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:23.170
With those as the baseline,
what we want to do now

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:26.620
is talk about
Apigee Edge platform

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:30.940
and give you a perspective of
what the platform does to allow

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:32.830
you to do what we just said.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:36.330
And before I get
there, this slide

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:39.580
talks about how
all of this plays

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:41.980
in the context of
additional value chain.

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:44.410
When you talk about
workloads, these workloads

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:46.210
can sometimes be
consuming applications.

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:48.760
These workloads can sometimes
be systems of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:51.544
But as long as you're able
to keep your APIs consistent,

00:07:51.544 --> 00:07:53.710
making sure that the clients
won't see a difference,

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:55.710
whether they're talking
with cloud-based backend

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:58.127
or an on-prem-based
backend, you're good.

00:07:58.127 --> 00:07:59.960
Similarly, you need to
secure all your APIs.

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:01.150
You need to make
them discoverable.

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:02.710
You need to make
them measurable.

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:07.930
So that's what Apigee is solving
for enterprise customers.

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:09.880
Before I get to
architecture patterns on how

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:12.280
we are doing that and the
common patterns we're seeing,

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:15.760
I wanted to talk a little bit
about what we do as a product,

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:18.280
and what are the
components involved,

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:23.215
and how they are allowing you
to do what we just talked about.

00:08:23.215 --> 00:08:27.520
At a very high
level, Apigee Edge,

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:30.010
it's the name of the product,
an API management platform.

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:31.010
It's one single product.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:34.230
You can conceptually think of
it as three different buckets

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:35.780
or three different categories.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:37.640
At the core, we
have API services

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:40.460
that is acting as
runtime sitting in

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:43.520
between your backend,
the systems of record,

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:45.260
and systems of engagement.

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:47.300
And then you have
developer services

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:49.280
that is allowing your
developers to get up

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:52.470
to speed, get on board, access
the API, play with the APIs,

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:55.530
understand the documentation,
so on and so forth.

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.890
And finally, there's analytics
that gives you visibility

00:08:57.890 --> 00:09:00.090
into how your APIs are doing.

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:04.050
So to double click a little bit
into what these components are,

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:06.435
the API services, like
I said, is the runtime.

00:09:06.435 --> 00:09:08.649
It's sitting in
between your systems

00:09:08.649 --> 00:09:09.690
of record, your backends.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:12.910
This could be, like I said,
your Kubernetes-based backends,

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:14.416
your VM-based backends.

00:09:14.416 --> 00:09:15.540
They could be on the cloud.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:16.456
They could be on-prem.

00:09:16.456 --> 00:09:18.340
But these are your
systems of record.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:20.790
And the clients
could be mobile apps,

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:24.360
could be partner integrations,
could be internal apps.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:28.330
They are talking to the backends
through this Apigee Edge

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:29.030
contact.

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:31.266
So think of Apigee
Edge as the proxy.

00:09:31.266 --> 00:09:32.640
The request come
from the client.

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:35.780
They come to these API
services, the runtime.

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:38.280
There, we enforce common
policies, common functionality,

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:42.732
like, let's say, security
or API key validation.

00:09:42.732 --> 00:09:44.440
And we do things like
traffic management,

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:47.430
making sure that no
single developer is

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:50.804
going beyond the quota that
they are allowed to access.

00:09:50.804 --> 00:09:53.220
Those are the kinds of things
that you want to do commonly

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.095
across your backends.

00:09:54.095 --> 00:09:57.360
And those are the policies,
or those are the functionality

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:01.500
that the runtime is enforcing
for you on the request pipeline

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:03.432
as the request comes
from the client.

00:10:03.432 --> 00:10:05.390
And finally, the request
is sent to the backed.

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:07.530
And now, in our
context, these backends

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:11.160
could be on-prem-based backends.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:14.460
They could be cloud-based
backends, but for the clients,

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:15.210
their transparent.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:16.884
So Apigee can take
it off routing.

00:10:16.884 --> 00:10:19.050
For example, even if you
have to move your workload.

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:22.970
Let's say you have
a SOAP-based backend

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:25.260
on some appliance or some VM.

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:29.460
And you want to move that to
a Kubernetes-based backend.

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:31.330
At the the runtime
level, Apigee can now

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:33.038
know that there is a
switch that happened

00:10:33.038 --> 00:10:34.050
in the system of record.

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:35.640
And we can make that transition.

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:38.302
And the client need not
go and rewrite his code,

00:10:38.302 --> 00:10:40.010
or they need not change
a bit of the code

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:45.810
that they have already written
to do what they're doing.

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:47.700
Similarly, on the
response part, we

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:50.360
do a lot of things like
response mediation.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:57.750
It's a very common scenario for
us to take a SOAP XML backend

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:00.030
and translate that to
an REST JSON backend.

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:02.800
That way, the clients are
comfortable consuming it.

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:07.230
And the backends can keep
updating the way they are.

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:13.310
So the summary is API services
is taking care of your APIs

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:14.890
from operations standpoint.

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:17.089
You're making your APIs
operationally ready.

00:11:17.089 --> 00:11:18.630
And once they're
operationally ready,

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:20.130
the second thing
that you want to do

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:24.810
as a developer is let other
developers access your APIs.

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:27.330
So that is where developer
services comes into picture.

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:29.430
This is generally a
white label developer

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:33.497
portal where your developers
will come to access the APIs.

00:11:33.497 --> 00:11:35.330
So they're coming into
the developer portal.

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:36.788
They do two, three
things commonly.

00:11:36.788 --> 00:11:39.150
One is they look
at documentation.

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:40.990
Nobody wants to
read documentation.

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:42.540
So developers
services takes care

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:48.720
of giving them a sandbox where
they can play with the APIs,

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:51.480
send a request, change
parameters, send a request,

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:53.230
look at what the
response would look like.

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:54.896
So they have complete
parity around what

00:11:54.896 --> 00:11:58.860
the API does before they
integrate this into their code.

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:00.750
The second thing that
developer services does

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.360
is it allows them
to access the API.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:04.560
They can sign up for a key.

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:06.595
They can sign up for a token.

00:12:06.595 --> 00:12:08.970
They can tell you what they're
trying to do with the API.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:10.230
And they can get access.

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:15.270
And they use that to actually
send requests, get responses.

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:18.240
In a sense, they entire
onboarding of your developers

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:19.377
can become self-service.

00:12:19.377 --> 00:12:20.960
It can help you in
two different ways.

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:24.599
One, if you are the developer,
you can put the API out there

00:12:24.599 --> 00:12:25.890
in the API management platform.

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:28.050
And you're not worried
about each team

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:29.790
that is trying to use the API.

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:34.210
If there are five different
teams that want to use the API,

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:38.860
they're not coming to
you five different times

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:41.350
to learn what the API does.

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:42.900
And two, from a
business standpoint,

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:45.720
once the APIs are out
there from multiple teams,

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:48.360
from multiple sources on
this developer portal,

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:50.910
you can go and make the
business partnerships.

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:52.639
You can onboard more channels.

00:12:52.639 --> 00:12:54.180
You can give them
access to the APIs.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:56.970
And they can come to the
portal and onboard themselves

00:12:56.970 --> 00:12:59.380
to learn about the APIs.

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:02.050
So that's the second
component of what we do.

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:03.470
One, it's API services.

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:04.390
That the runtime.

00:13:04.390 --> 00:13:06.322
Making your APIs
operationally ready.

00:13:06.322 --> 00:13:07.780
Second is the
developer portal that

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:11.390
is making the consumption
of these APIs easier.

00:13:11.390 --> 00:13:13.300
So the net result
of these two is

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:16.741
you have teams of people who are
building APIs, onboarding APIs.

00:13:16.741 --> 00:13:19.240
And you have teams of developers
who are consuming the APIs.

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:22.289
And generally speaking,
these teams are not the same.

00:13:22.289 --> 00:13:23.830
They're not sitting
in the same room.

00:13:23.830 --> 00:13:24.910
They're not sitting
in the same office.

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:27.010
And sometimes, the
consuming developers

00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:28.180
are outside your enterprise.

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:29.840
They could be your partners.

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:34.030
So analytics
becomes your insight

00:13:34.030 --> 00:13:36.410
into how your APIs are
being consumed if you are

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:38.542
a developer who owns that API.

00:13:38.542 --> 00:13:40.000
And if you're a
business owner, you

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:42.220
can look at how your
channels are doing.

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:44.860
Which channels are driving you
more traffic, where they're

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:46.600
coming from, all
the standard things

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:48.130
that you want to
look at to measure

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:49.450
develop your API program.

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:52.540
So that's the reason
why we have analytics

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:57.650
as a first-class citizen in the
entire API management platform.

00:13:57.650 --> 00:13:59.792
So those are the three
things at a very high level.

00:13:59.792 --> 00:14:01.000
And there's a lot more depth.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:02.530
We'll cover those
in other webinars,

00:14:02.530 --> 00:14:06.220
so you can learn
them in the follow-up

00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:08.740
webinars or the content
that we put online.

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:12.310
But these are the
three things that

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:14.920
constitute an API
management platform

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:17.290
that we are as Apigee Edge.

00:14:17.290 --> 00:14:21.622
And in the context of a cloud
acceleration or migration,

00:14:21.622 --> 00:14:23.080
these three become
super important.

00:14:23.080 --> 00:14:27.450
Because now, in addition
to your existing backends,

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:29.080
you're now introducing
new concepts

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:33.140
of cloud-based backends, the
manage services that you're

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:37.150
using, the containers, the
Kubernetes-based backends.

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:42.610
All of these will have
to be made transparent

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:44.680
for the clients for consuming.

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:47.740
So that's the context
of how Apigee Edge is

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:52.490
used in cloud migration
acceleration projects.

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:55.070
So what I'll do now,
now that we looked

00:14:55.070 --> 00:14:57.580
at the common themes of
what customers are doing

00:14:57.580 --> 00:15:00.550
with respect to
the cloud adoption

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:03.400
and how they're using containers
in the context of that,

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:05.820
and then we looked at
Apigee Edge as a product,

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:09.610
I'll walk you through three
common architectural patterns

00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:11.560
that we're seeing
as customers embrace

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:13.120
their journey towards Cloud.

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:16.040
You can almost think of
them as a progression

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:18.340
or three different
scenarios that

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:21.160
fit together in the
maturity of enterprise

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:22.510
adoption towards Cloud.

00:15:22.510 --> 00:15:25.390
The first pattern that
you see here on the slide

00:15:25.390 --> 00:15:31.220
is an area where an enterprise
customer is mostly on-prem.

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:32.460
They have VMs.

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:33.460
They have Kubernetes.

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:37.930
They have other services
that are on-premise.

00:15:37.930 --> 00:15:39.420
The primary mode
of infrastructure

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:41.410
is essentially on-prem.

00:15:41.410 --> 00:15:45.070
But they want to use
Cloud for a new project,

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:46.130
for a niche project.

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:49.240
It could be, as an example,
here is a customer who

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:52.450
has most of their software,
most of their IT on-prem,

00:15:52.450 --> 00:15:55.750
but they're looking to run
a prediction using a tensor

00:15:55.750 --> 00:15:57.800
flow on GCP.

00:15:57.800 --> 00:16:00.370
Now, they want to use
GCP for that one project.

00:16:00.370 --> 00:16:03.666
But the prediction that comes
out of that tensor flow model

00:16:03.666 --> 00:16:06.040
will have to be made accessible
by other on-prem systems.

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:09.580
Because that is how your
websites are being served.

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:11.800
That's where your mobile
apps are being served.

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:14.650
So the final output
of that prediction

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:18.400
could be an API that
has to be consumed

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:20.020
by the on-prem systems.

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:23.137
That's the problem statement
in which Apigee acts here,

00:16:23.137 --> 00:16:24.970
or that's the problem
that Apigee is solving

00:16:24.970 --> 00:16:26.480
for enterprise customers.

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:28.650
There are two, three
things that we do commonly.

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:33.892
One, as you talk to
your new project in GCP,

00:16:33.892 --> 00:16:35.350
you don't want your
on-prem systems

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:40.600
to learn new authentication
models or to, let's say,

00:16:40.600 --> 00:16:44.980
do a service account-based
authentication that you

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:47.570
may normally have in
your cloud projects.

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:50.140
So what you may want to do is
integrate with your existing

00:16:50.140 --> 00:16:52.869
identity provider, or IDP.

00:16:52.869 --> 00:16:54.660
So in that case, Apigee
could be that layer

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:56.830
that is talking to
you IDP, making sure

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:59.260
that the calls that
you're making to the Cloud

00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:01.190
platform or the
tensor flow model,

00:17:01.190 --> 00:17:04.000
in this case, the prediction
API are being authenticated

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:07.660
against your IDP the same
way that your other systems,

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:09.099
other mobile apps,
other web apps

00:17:09.099 --> 00:17:12.790
are consuming [INAUDIBLE]
APIs [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:12.790 --> 00:17:16.359
The second thing that customers
want to use Apigee for

00:17:16.359 --> 00:17:18.430
is enforcement of policies.

00:17:18.430 --> 00:17:21.106
Sometimes, especially
when you're

00:17:21.106 --> 00:17:22.480
doing your first,
second projects

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:24.970
on the Cloud platform
on the cloud,

00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:28.320
you don't want sensitive data to
go out of your on-prem systems.

00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:30.070
So now, in this case,
we're talking about,

00:17:30.070 --> 00:17:31.750
let's say, a prediction API.

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:34.150
And the request of
the prediction API,

00:17:34.150 --> 00:17:38.970
as an enterprise customer, you
want to control what goes in.

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:42.310
The post payload should not
contain any sensitive data.

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:46.087
If that is a use case
that you want to solve,

00:17:46.087 --> 00:17:47.920
again, Apigee can be
your enforcement point.

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:50.305
There, we can make sure
that the request that

00:17:50.305 --> 00:17:53.350
goes into this prediction
API are not sensitive data.

00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:58.240
We can enforce things like data
loss prevention, regex checks,

00:17:58.240 --> 00:17:59.740
the common things
that people want

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:04.300
to do to ensure that
this tensor data is not

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:06.630
going into the Cloud platform.

00:18:06.630 --> 00:18:10.374
So in essence, what happens
here is enterprise customers

00:18:10.374 --> 00:18:11.790
are now comfortable
that they have

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:13.710
a layer that's acting
as a gatekeeper,

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:17.090
so they can get started
with their initial project

00:18:17.090 --> 00:18:18.180
on the cloud.

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:21.350
So once they do that, the second
architecture that we're seeing,

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:23.130
again, think of it
as a progression.

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:25.213
Now you've done your first,
second, third projects

00:18:25.213 --> 00:18:25.900
on the cloud.

00:18:25.900 --> 00:18:27.810
Now you want to move some
of your existing workloads.

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:29.643
You want to lift and
shift some of your VMs,

00:18:29.643 --> 00:18:34.770
or you want to modernize
and containerized them

00:18:34.770 --> 00:18:36.355
into something like GKE.

00:18:36.355 --> 00:18:37.980
So if you're doing
something like that,

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:41.250
again, Apigee becomes a crucial
piece as an abstraction layer

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:43.360
that we're talking about today.

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:45.640
And the things that we do
can be slightly different.

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:48.662
For example, as you
move the workloads,

00:18:48.662 --> 00:18:51.120
there are two or three problems
that we're seeing commonly.

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:54.570
One, the amount of
authentication schemes

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:56.792
that come into
picture increases.

00:18:56.792 --> 00:18:58.500
Let's say, on-prem,
you may be doing two,

00:18:58.500 --> 00:18:59.340
three, authentication models.

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:01.050
It could be a SAML-based
authentication.

00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:03.383
It could be a web-service
security-based authentication.

00:19:03.383 --> 00:19:05.250
It could be two or three others.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:07.640
And now, once you
move to GCP, you

00:19:07.640 --> 00:19:09.930
will end up with something
like a service account

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:13.870
with authentication, App Engine
firewalls, or the mutual SSL.

00:19:13.870 --> 00:19:17.280
So managing that
becomes a tricky thing.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:19.950
The second thing that happens
is you cannot move everything

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:20.590
at once.

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:22.980
Let's say you picked the
workload that you want to move.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:26.800
There could be parts
of that workload,

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:28.880
there could be an appliance,
that you cannot move

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:30.240
immediately to the cloud.

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:32.292
So whatever you're
lifting and shifting--

00:19:32.292 --> 00:19:33.750
could be a VM that
you're shifting,

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:37.620
or could be a modernized,
containerized GKE workload that

00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:39.150
you're moving to Cloud--

00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:43.230
that has to inevitably access
something or the other on-prem.

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:48.180
And that's the area where Apigee
can again solve and make sure

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:53.310
that your cloud backends and
accessing whatever backends

00:19:53.310 --> 00:19:57.480
that you cannot move on day one
or move at all and give you one

00:19:57.480 --> 00:20:01.170
consistent experience on how you
consume these applications from

00:20:01.170 --> 00:20:04.170
a cloud standpoint.

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:06.970
And similarly, unifying
security, for example,

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:09.270
in this slide, we're talking
about the scenario where

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:11.353
your backend may have a
SAML-based authentication,

00:20:11.353 --> 00:20:14.040
but you are consuming the
structure of GKE applications

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:14.770
using joud--

00:20:14.770 --> 00:20:18.340
J-O-U-D-- validation.

00:20:18.340 --> 00:20:20.340
So you want to consume
the on-prem the same way.

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:25.700
So you want to send a joud token
and access the on-prem systems.

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:27.380
But the on-prem knows only SAML.

00:20:27.380 --> 00:20:30.569
So Apigee is the layer that is
acting as a mediation engine,

00:20:30.569 --> 00:20:32.610
making sure that those
translations are happening

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:33.560
properly.

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:35.530
Similarly, the use case
that I talked about

00:20:35.530 --> 00:20:37.530
is data translations--

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:39.329
SOAP to REST, XML to JSON.

00:20:39.329 --> 00:20:41.370
You may want to consumer
a JSON, but your backend

00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:42.600
is exposing an XML.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:44.190
So what do you do?

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:47.790
You use Apigee as a mediation
layer or the API Gateway

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:50.770
to get the information
that you are looking for.

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:52.192
So that's the second step.

00:20:52.192 --> 00:20:53.650
Once you do these
two successfully,

00:20:53.650 --> 00:20:56.410
you have initial projects,
and you've proved success

00:20:56.410 --> 00:20:58.360
with your cloud journey.

00:20:58.360 --> 00:21:00.010
And then you are
comfortable moving

00:21:00.010 --> 00:21:02.770
some of your existing workloads,
lift them and shift them

00:21:02.770 --> 00:21:03.467
to the cloud.

00:21:03.467 --> 00:21:05.050
And then, you come
to this final state

00:21:05.050 --> 00:21:07.799
where you have a hybrid cloud.

00:21:07.799 --> 00:21:09.840
You have a scenario where
you have workloads that

00:21:09.840 --> 00:21:13.720
are across on-prem and cloud.

00:21:13.720 --> 00:21:17.950
You could use solutions like
Google Cloud Interconnect

00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:22.150
or VPC [INAUDIBLE] to
make sure that the data is

00:21:22.150 --> 00:21:25.400
accessible to these two
from the backend standpoint.

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:26.902
But for external
consumption, they

00:21:26.902 --> 00:21:28.360
do not want to know
the difference.

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:32.016
They should not care where
the backends are running.

00:21:35.020 --> 00:21:41.170
If you have a backend that has
one workload on GKE and one

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:44.139
workload on-prem, on
Kubernetes, your clients

00:21:44.139 --> 00:21:45.680
will not want to
know the difference.

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:47.054
They don't want
to send one token

00:21:47.054 --> 00:21:49.420
to a GKE-based backend
and another token

00:21:49.420 --> 00:21:52.130
to an on-prem based
Kubernetes backend.

00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:55.360
They want to unify that
across the backends.

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:58.240
Let's say you also
want to integrate that

00:21:58.240 --> 00:22:02.520
with your existing IDP, like an
LDAP or a cloud-based identity

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:06.050
provider that you're
leveraging internally.

00:22:06.050 --> 00:22:08.390
So those are the things
that Apigee can solve.

00:22:08.390 --> 00:22:10.752
And then there could be
higher level functions.

00:22:10.752 --> 00:22:12.460
You may want to monetize
some of the APIs

00:22:12.460 --> 00:22:13.810
that you're putting out there.

00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:16.410
So Apigee can be the
layer where you are

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:17.790
applying the [INAUDIBLE] plans.

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:19.867
You are applying the
monetization plans.

00:22:19.867 --> 00:22:20.950
You charged the developer.

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:22.783
So you pay the developers
for using the API.

00:22:22.783 --> 00:22:24.760
And that should be
uniform across wherever

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:28.190
the workloads are coming from.

00:22:28.190 --> 00:22:30.790
We talked about developer
portals, developer services.

00:22:30.790 --> 00:22:32.600
You put up one single portal.

00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:36.350
So you put all the APIs from
all these different backends.

00:22:36.350 --> 00:22:41.180
Developers or your partners
are just looking at one portal.

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:46.370
All of the implementation detail
is kind of hidden from them.

00:22:46.370 --> 00:22:47.990
So that's a third
use case, really,

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:51.020
where, once you get to
the true hybrid scenario,

00:22:51.020 --> 00:22:53.720
you have Apigee acting
as your entry point

00:22:53.720 --> 00:22:56.060
across all your
backends, making sure

00:22:56.060 --> 00:22:59.650
that you have one consistent
unified experience on how

00:22:59.650 --> 00:23:02.510
your APIs are being consumed
by internal developers,

00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:05.710
by external developers,
and by your partners.

00:23:05.710 --> 00:23:07.430
Those are the three patterns.

00:23:07.430 --> 00:23:10.610
One, start with a Greenfield.

00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:13.340
Pick up the first use case that
you want to use in the cloud.

00:23:13.340 --> 00:23:14.960
Apigee can make
sure that whatever

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:16.940
you're doing in the
cloud in that project

00:23:16.940 --> 00:23:19.470
is accessible to
your on-prem systems.

00:23:19.470 --> 00:23:22.310
So you have that integrated.

00:23:22.310 --> 00:23:23.600
And the second use case--

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:27.350
once you're comfortable with
one, two, three projects,

00:23:27.350 --> 00:23:29.782
you move some of your
workloads to Cloud.

00:23:29.782 --> 00:23:31.240
Apigee is making
sure that whatever

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:33.489
you are moving to Cloud, if
it has to access something

00:23:33.489 --> 00:23:35.100
developer, you can do that.

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:39.750
And if it has to do mediations,
do data translations,

00:23:39.750 --> 00:23:41.660
you do that on the Apigee layer.

00:23:41.660 --> 00:23:44.750
So you build the
code the way you do.

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:46.230
You containerize it.

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:48.102
You lift and shift
the way you want to.

00:23:48.102 --> 00:23:50.060
In the third use case,
you have a hybrid cloud.

00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:51.870
You have customers
coming from outside.

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:54.060
You have other
teams using the API.

00:23:54.060 --> 00:23:56.480
You give them one single
consistent experience

00:23:56.480 --> 00:24:01.640
while you keep changing where
the workloads are running.

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:04.320
Those are the three patterns
that are very high level.

00:24:04.320 --> 00:24:10.070
So what I'll do now is jump
from the slides to a demo.

00:24:10.070 --> 00:24:12.130
But before I do that,
I wanted to do us

00:24:12.130 --> 00:24:15.530
a quick context of what we'll be
demoing in the context of what

00:24:15.530 --> 00:24:17.780
we just spoke about.

00:24:17.780 --> 00:24:19.910
For the demo, this
is a set up slide.

00:24:19.910 --> 00:24:23.280
I do have a VM that
has a hotel's API.

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:25.090
The demo, I'm taking
a use case where

00:24:25.090 --> 00:24:26.750
it's a hospitality company.

00:24:26.750 --> 00:24:28.680
They have information
about a bunch of hotels.

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:30.260
You look at the
hotel information.

00:24:30.260 --> 00:24:32.120
You look at the
ratings, reviews.

00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:33.560
And then you select the hotel.

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:35.030
And you can do the booking.

00:24:35.030 --> 00:24:37.040
So that's the backend
that we're talking about.

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:38.665
So traditionally,
think of the scenario

00:24:38.665 --> 00:24:41.730
where they have built
all of that on their VMs.

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:47.370
And they want to move
some of those to the GKE

00:24:47.370 --> 00:24:50.150
to manage Kubernetes, and
it's the easiest place

00:24:50.150 --> 00:24:55.220
where you can containerize
and shift your workloads.

00:24:55.220 --> 00:24:57.846
In this use case, they're not
moving everything at once.

00:24:57.846 --> 00:24:59.720
They're picking the
hotel catalog information

00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:03.290
and moving that to GKE and
rest of it will stay on VM.

00:25:03.290 --> 00:25:07.179
And even that, as you are
going through the journey,

00:25:07.179 --> 00:25:08.720
the scenario that
we're talking about

00:25:08.720 --> 00:25:12.830
is think of this is a
customer who wants to do

00:25:12.830 --> 00:25:15.220
or a kind of redeployment
or kind of reupgrade.

00:25:15.220 --> 00:25:18.290
They don't want to move all the
clients to a GKE-based backend

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:19.220
once they build it.

00:25:19.220 --> 00:25:23.390
They want to try that
with the [INAUDIBLE]

00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:24.885
maybe internal applications.

00:25:24.885 --> 00:25:26.510
And then once they
are comfortable they

00:25:26.510 --> 00:25:29.567
know how the process works,
how the scaling works,

00:25:29.567 --> 00:25:31.400
then they will move the
rest of the clients.

00:25:31.400 --> 00:25:33.970
So they're using Apigee
as an abstraction layer.

00:25:33.970 --> 00:25:36.110
They're deciding
what should go to GKE

00:25:36.110 --> 00:25:37.560
and what should go to VMs.

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:41.000
And that's the demo
we'll be talking about.

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:45.660
And remember, the use case
really is just workload demo.

00:25:45.660 --> 00:25:48.500
As for the workloads, like
payments, like reservations.

00:25:48.500 --> 00:25:51.920
they'll continue being
on VMs until they're

00:25:51.920 --> 00:25:55.047
comfortable moving those also.

00:25:55.047 --> 00:25:56.380
That's the context for the demo.

00:25:56.380 --> 00:25:58.540
So now, what I'll do is
I'll switch my screen

00:25:58.540 --> 00:26:02.670
and show you the interfaces.

00:26:02.670 --> 00:26:04.870
I'll be walking you through
two or three interfaces.

00:26:04.870 --> 00:26:08.970
One interface is the Cloud
platform, the Cloud console,

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:12.695
which is where how my VMs or how
my Kubernetes-based backends.

00:26:12.695 --> 00:26:14.820
And the second interface
that I'll walk you through

00:26:14.820 --> 00:26:16.480
is the Apigee interface.

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:19.050
This is the place where you're
managing the APIs, where

00:26:19.050 --> 00:26:24.330
you are doing the routing,
enforcing policies, and looking

00:26:24.330 --> 00:26:27.307
at analytics, and generally
managing the APIs.

00:26:27.307 --> 00:26:29.640
So these are the thing that
I'll be walking you through.

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:32.490
And of course, I do have my own
application, the APIs that I've

00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:36.120
created, and the UI
that consumes the hotel

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:39.180
data that I was talking about.

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:43.110
What you have here
is a bunch of VMs

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:48.390
that are acting as the backend
for my hotel's base backed.

00:26:48.390 --> 00:26:50.060
And I have a
spring-based app that

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:53.550
is serving information about
hotels catalog, reservations,

00:26:53.550 --> 00:26:54.300
payments.

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:57.300
All of that is driven by the
spring application talking

00:26:57.300 --> 00:27:00.264
to my SQL in the back.

00:27:00.264 --> 00:27:01.680
Generally speaking,
in production,

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:03.554
you would probably have
three or four or five

00:27:03.554 --> 00:27:07.290
of these VMs to give you the
resilience high availability.

00:27:07.290 --> 00:27:11.010
And you probably
will [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:11.010 --> 00:27:13.170
the capacity for your peak,
even though the peak is

00:27:13.170 --> 00:27:16.220
happening maybe one or
two months in a bad year.

00:27:16.220 --> 00:27:17.950
That's a common scenario anyway.

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:20.580
So here, I have this VM.

00:27:20.580 --> 00:27:22.810
If I access this
[? /hotels ?] API,

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:24.730
you will see that I
get an array of hotels.

00:27:28.470 --> 00:27:30.270
Each of the elements
in the array

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:32.790
is telling me information
about one single hotel--

00:27:32.790 --> 00:27:38.180
the ratings, reviews, links
to where they're coming from.

00:27:38.180 --> 00:27:38.930
That's my backend.

00:27:43.410 --> 00:27:48.560
This is the shell
prompt for my VM.

00:27:48.560 --> 00:27:54.260
So what I'm doing now is taking
this particular API I just

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:56.000
showed you-- the
hotel's catalogue--

00:27:56.000 --> 00:27:58.200
and moving that to
a GKE-based backend.

00:28:03.550 --> 00:28:10.230
What I've done is taken an
existing spring application,

00:28:10.230 --> 00:28:12.837
and then taken a piece
of that, and taken

00:28:12.837 --> 00:28:13.920
that piece of spring code.

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:16.260
And I'm using cloud
SQL in the backend.

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:22.860
Because once I containerize,
I can leverage these factors

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:24.540
that I can attach
to my application.

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:29.010
And the cloud SQL can probably
scale my data much better

00:28:29.010 --> 00:28:32.910
than what I could do on my own
on my VM-based infrastructure.

00:28:32.910 --> 00:28:36.682
So now, if I do the
[INAUDIBLE] services,

00:28:36.682 --> 00:28:38.640
you will see that I have
two services that I've

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:40.274
deployed on Kubernetes.

00:28:40.274 --> 00:28:42.690
One of them is the hotel's
API, which is just the catalogs

00:28:42.690 --> 00:28:43.710
API and nothing else.

00:28:43.710 --> 00:28:46.830
The rest-- the payments, the
reservations-- all of that

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:51.450
is staying back with the
VM-based infrastructure

00:28:51.450 --> 00:28:53.070
that I was just showing you.

00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:55.740
And then I do have
the hotel's UI

00:28:55.740 --> 00:28:59.020
that is serving as
the UI for this demo.

00:28:59.020 --> 00:29:01.230
So before I show the
UI, I just wanted

00:29:01.230 --> 00:29:02.880
to quickly show you the hotels.

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:05.670
There are two things that you
may notice as a difference.

00:29:05.670 --> 00:29:11.560
One is, as I am, let's say,
modernizing or containerizing

00:29:11.560 --> 00:29:13.910
my application, I have added
a version number to my app,

00:29:13.910 --> 00:29:14.900
to my API.

00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:17.504
So my API URLs slightly
look different.

00:29:17.504 --> 00:29:19.170
And the second thing
that you may notice

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:27.110
is the resource structure
itself is slightly different.

00:29:27.110 --> 00:29:30.950
For example, in
my other backend,

00:29:30.950 --> 00:29:34.790
I have the hotels
as a simply JSON.

00:29:34.790 --> 00:29:37.789
But as I built a
second version, I

00:29:37.789 --> 00:29:39.080
have an element called portals.

00:29:39.080 --> 00:29:42.470
And inside that is an array
that shows the hotel's data.

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:47.900
These changes are expected.

00:29:47.900 --> 00:29:50.570
You may notice that as good
you're developing applications,

00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:53.690
as you are upgrading your
API versions, some of these

00:29:53.690 --> 00:29:55.160
are natural.

00:29:55.160 --> 00:29:58.114
But the most important thing
is you do not want your clients

00:29:58.114 --> 00:29:59.030
to see the difference.

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:02.780
That's the part that Apigee's
solving for you, which

00:30:02.780 --> 00:30:04.710
I'm going to show you in a bit.

00:30:04.710 --> 00:30:06.140
But before that, this is my UI.

00:30:06.140 --> 00:30:13.400
It's a simple UI, where from
a contradiction standpoint,

00:30:13.400 --> 00:30:15.530
I will give my API
key that I want

00:30:15.530 --> 00:30:18.080
to use, and send a request,
and it will give me information

00:30:18.080 --> 00:30:19.310
about all the hotels.

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:23.780
It's a very simple UI.

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:26.430
This information could be coming
from one, or two, or three

00:30:26.430 --> 00:30:27.530
backends.

00:30:27.530 --> 00:30:29.210
For example, when
I click on book,

00:30:29.210 --> 00:30:31.760
this could be going to
the reservations API.

00:30:31.760 --> 00:30:33.652
That is being served
by the springboard app.

00:30:33.652 --> 00:30:36.110
Whereas this information, the
catalogue information itself,

00:30:36.110 --> 00:30:39.080
is coming from a
GKE-based packet.

00:30:39.080 --> 00:30:40.910
So now, I talked
about a scenario

00:30:40.910 --> 00:30:44.700
where the client wants to do
a [INAUDIBLE] based upgrade.

00:30:44.700 --> 00:30:46.880
They do not want to move
all the clients at once.

00:30:46.880 --> 00:30:50.450
So they're using
Apigee to decide

00:30:50.450 --> 00:30:52.110
which ones they want to move.

00:30:52.110 --> 00:30:54.856
For example, if I use
this particular API key,

00:30:54.856 --> 00:30:57.170
Apigee will know that
this is an app that

00:30:57.170 --> 00:31:00.180
has to go to a GKE-based packet
and will route the request

00:31:00.180 --> 00:31:01.190
accordingly.

00:31:01.190 --> 00:31:03.620
Whereas, if I use
another API key,

00:31:03.620 --> 00:31:05.900
there is an application
attribute for this key.

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:08.420
And when Apigee
looks at this, it

00:31:08.420 --> 00:31:14.710
knows that this has to go to
a VM-based spring backend.

00:31:14.710 --> 00:31:17.869
When I send a request, it
goes to the VM-based backend.

00:31:17.869 --> 00:31:19.910
But as a developer, you
won't see the difference.

00:31:19.910 --> 00:31:21.380
We talked about the
fact that the APIs

00:31:21.380 --> 00:31:22.640
could be slightly different.

00:31:22.640 --> 00:31:24.590
All of that is hidden.

00:31:24.590 --> 00:31:29.060
You'll want this UI
code to access these two

00:31:29.060 --> 00:31:32.762
APIs separately and render
them in different ways.

00:31:32.762 --> 00:31:34.970
So those are the kinds of
things that Apigee is doing

00:31:34.970 --> 00:31:36.740
for you behind the scenes.

00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:39.130
So what I'll do now is
switch to the Apigee UI

00:31:39.130 --> 00:31:43.850
to show you what's
happening behind the scenes,

00:31:43.850 --> 00:31:46.670
as I'm doing what I
was doing in the UI.

00:31:46.670 --> 00:31:49.050
So what I have here
is the trace window.

00:31:49.050 --> 00:31:52.830
So for example, when I was
sending those two requests,

00:31:52.830 --> 00:31:53.910
this is my first request.

00:31:53.910 --> 00:31:55.400
And this is my second request.

00:31:55.400 --> 00:31:58.970
So the second request, what
you're seeing specifically

00:31:58.970 --> 00:32:01.640
on the screen is what we
call as a trace window.

00:32:01.640 --> 00:32:03.470
This is your debugging tool.

00:32:03.470 --> 00:32:05.930
This is the place you come to
when you want to introspect

00:32:05.930 --> 00:32:07.360
what's happening with the API.

00:32:07.360 --> 00:32:10.640
But in the context
of the UI, the UI

00:32:10.640 --> 00:32:13.340
sent a request with slash
we want slash hotels

00:32:13.340 --> 00:32:14.954
and with the API key.

00:32:14.954 --> 00:32:16.370
And the first thing
we're doing is

00:32:16.370 --> 00:32:18.230
we're making sure that
the API key is valid

00:32:18.230 --> 00:32:21.770
and this API key can access
this hotel's resource.

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:24.290
And it's not an invalid key.

00:32:24.290 --> 00:32:27.560
So now, in addition to doing
that, what we are also doing

00:32:27.560 --> 00:32:30.710
is we're picking up a lot of
these variables that could be

00:32:30.710 --> 00:32:32.420
application-specific variables.

00:32:32.420 --> 00:32:34.910
And one of those
variables is telling me

00:32:34.910 --> 00:32:39.230
that this particular application
has a configuration that

00:32:39.230 --> 00:32:42.290
says that the request should
go to the VM-based backend.

00:32:42.290 --> 00:32:46.814
And that is an attribute
called API key dot backend.

00:32:46.814 --> 00:32:48.230
And once I look
at that attribute,

00:32:48.230 --> 00:32:50.420
I'm doing a routing
based on that.

00:32:50.420 --> 00:32:52.520
So there's a route
condition that matched.

00:32:52.520 --> 00:32:55.040
And now' I know that I have to
send it to a VM-based backed.

00:32:55.040 --> 00:32:58.100
And what you will notice is we
are sending it to slash hotels,

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:01.280
even though the request came
to slash we want slash hotels.

00:33:01.280 --> 00:33:03.634
And this is what the
backend responded back with.

00:33:03.634 --> 00:33:05.800
And as you have seen before,
this is a simple array.

00:33:05.800 --> 00:33:07.610
It doesn't have the
hotel's element.

00:33:07.610 --> 00:33:09.405
And without that,
the UI will fail.

00:33:09.405 --> 00:33:11.780
So you'll see that there is
a policy here on the response

00:33:11.780 --> 00:33:15.110
pipeline where we're
taking the response that

00:33:15.110 --> 00:33:18.440
was given to us by the
backend and massaging that

00:33:18.440 --> 00:33:20.960
into something that
the UI will understand.

00:33:20.960 --> 00:33:23.630
In this case, it's a simple
JavaScript per JavaScript

00:33:23.630 --> 00:33:24.200
translation.

00:33:24.200 --> 00:33:28.190
But we have seen more things are
done on Apigee, like a SOAP XML

00:33:28.190 --> 00:33:33.530
to [INAUDIBLE] translation, or
even talking to two or three

00:33:33.530 --> 00:33:35.600
backends behind the scenes
and aggregating them

00:33:35.600 --> 00:33:38.390
into one single unified API.

00:33:38.390 --> 00:33:40.070
So that's the trace window.

00:33:40.070 --> 00:33:42.350
But as an API
management platform,

00:33:42.350 --> 00:33:44.130
you do get a lot
of other things.

00:33:44.130 --> 00:33:48.920
For example, you can
look at all the APIs

00:33:48.920 --> 00:33:50.420
that came to your system.

00:33:50.420 --> 00:33:52.520
And then you can measure
what's happening.

00:33:52.520 --> 00:33:55.580
You can see which
developer applications.

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:58.220
Let's say you see a
sudden spike on your API

00:33:58.220 --> 00:33:59.520
at this point in time.

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:01.610
You can ask Apigee
to tell you what's

00:34:01.610 --> 00:34:04.590
happening at that point in time,
which developer application is

00:34:04.590 --> 00:34:05.270
sending traffic.

00:34:05.270 --> 00:34:06.620
Is it a valid one?

00:34:06.620 --> 00:34:08.630
Is it invalid one?

00:34:08.630 --> 00:34:10.124
Should you enforce more quotas?

00:34:10.124 --> 00:34:11.540
Those are the
information that you

00:34:11.540 --> 00:34:13.520
want to know as an API owner.

00:34:13.520 --> 00:34:15.980
And for example,
if are suspecting

00:34:15.980 --> 00:34:20.246
that there could be a specific
client IP address that

00:34:20.246 --> 00:34:21.620
is sending a
[INAUDIBLE] request,

00:34:21.620 --> 00:34:22.870
you can look at the analytics.

00:34:22.870 --> 00:34:26.400
It will tell you where the
requests are coming from.

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:29.420
And if you see that there is
an invalid IP address that

00:34:29.420 --> 00:34:33.650
is sending a bunch of requests,
you can block them right there.

00:34:33.650 --> 00:34:36.320
So those other things that
you do from an API management

00:34:36.320 --> 00:34:37.699
standpoint.

00:34:37.699 --> 00:34:39.980
One-- use the policies.

00:34:39.980 --> 00:34:41.989
I was showing you
the trace, but these

00:34:41.989 --> 00:34:43.730
are the full set of
policies that you can

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:45.980
use from a platform standpoint.

00:34:45.980 --> 00:34:48.980
For example, you can add a spike
in this policy and say that,

00:34:48.980 --> 00:34:51.170
any request going to
my VM-based backed,

00:34:51.170 --> 00:34:54.109
I know that it cannot scale as
well as my GKE-based backend.

00:34:54.109 --> 00:34:56.150
So let's take that and
make sure that it does not

00:34:56.150 --> 00:35:00.130
get more than 100
requests per second.

00:35:00.130 --> 00:35:04.210
We talked about things
like DLP, regex checks--

00:35:04.210 --> 00:35:07.440
all these are available for
you from a security standpoint.

00:35:07.440 --> 00:35:09.730
And if you want to do
things like mediation,

00:35:09.730 --> 00:35:14.140
translating XML to JSON,
XML to XML, JSON to JSON,

00:35:14.140 --> 00:35:15.640
all those translations
or mediations

00:35:15.640 --> 00:35:17.280
are something that you can do.

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:19.939
And also, you can
go to the extent of,

00:35:19.939 --> 00:35:21.480
let's say, plugging
in your own code.

00:35:21.480 --> 00:35:23.438
You can write your own
Java code or [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:23.438 --> 00:35:28.640
code or JavaScript
code to tell what

00:35:28.640 --> 00:35:31.030
should be done when request
comes from the client

00:35:31.030 --> 00:35:34.920
or when the response is
going back to the client.

00:35:34.920 --> 00:35:37.110
That's a quick demo
of the product.

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:39.870
Again, there's a lot
more to the product.

00:35:39.870 --> 00:35:42.990
Once you make your APIs
operationally ready,

00:35:42.990 --> 00:35:44.280
you can put the documentation.

00:35:44.280 --> 00:35:46.821
You can put a developer portal
where your developers come to.

00:35:46.821 --> 00:35:49.620
They need not see any of this
or any of the infrastructure

00:35:49.620 --> 00:35:50.650
underneath.

00:35:50.650 --> 00:35:55.500
They look at a single website,
which is your developer portal,

00:35:55.500 --> 00:35:57.840
and they use that
to access the APIs.

00:35:57.840 --> 00:36:00.930
So all of that information
is available for you online.

00:36:00.930 --> 00:36:02.700
You can try with the product.

00:36:02.700 --> 00:36:06.450
You can look at
additional scenarios

00:36:06.450 --> 00:36:09.990
on where API management
can become useful

00:36:09.990 --> 00:36:13.920
in the context of a full
[INAUDIBLE] API management.

00:36:13.920 --> 00:36:16.440
But today, I want to talk in
the context of how this API

00:36:16.440 --> 00:36:19.920
management platform can
allow you to move workloads

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:24.900
across on-prem cloud by
acting as an abstraction layer

00:36:24.900 --> 00:36:29.470
to give you the flexibility
to move things around.

00:36:29.470 --> 00:36:34.920
So that's the quick demo on
how API management platform is

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:38.820
solving one specific use
case and that use case is

00:36:38.820 --> 00:36:41.091
moving workloads from
cloud to on-prem.

00:36:41.091 --> 00:36:43.590
And in the process, you may
want to modernize and move them,

00:36:43.590 --> 00:36:45.048
or you may just
want to [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:45.048 --> 00:36:47.580
an existing VM to GCE.

00:36:47.580 --> 00:36:50.550
But the abstraction layer
is important for enterprises

00:36:50.550 --> 00:36:55.110
to get that flexibility, to
get that assurance that things

00:36:55.110 --> 00:36:58.380
won't break, things won't
get impacted for people

00:36:58.380 --> 00:37:01.150
who are consuming them.

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:02.970
So where can you go from here?

