00:38:53.780 --> 00:38:56.020
The first question
is, can I use Apigee

00:38:56.020 --> 00:39:00.920
even if I'm not using
Kubernetes or GKE?

00:39:00.920 --> 00:39:02.280
Yeah, absolutely.

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:04.830
Like I was showing in
that architect slide,

00:39:04.830 --> 00:39:06.440
people sometimes
want to modernize

00:39:06.440 --> 00:39:10.470
and sometimes they want to
modernize in other ways.

00:39:10.470 --> 00:39:13.260
Kubernetes or not,
GKE or not, there's

00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:14.850
a need for API management.

00:39:14.850 --> 00:39:16.740
And there's a need
to move workloads

00:39:16.740 --> 00:39:21.440
around that is abstracted
from whoever is using it

00:39:21.440 --> 00:39:23.067
or abstracted from the client.

00:39:23.067 --> 00:39:23.650
So absolutely.

00:39:23.650 --> 00:39:26.260
You can Apigee, even if you're
not using Kubernetes or GKE.

00:39:28.940 --> 00:39:35.090
So the second question here
is, I do not have mobile apps.

00:39:35.090 --> 00:39:36.170
Is Apigee still relevant?

00:39:39.670 --> 00:39:42.450
Yeah, sure, absolutely.

00:39:42.450 --> 00:39:43.910
This is a common
question for us.

00:39:43.910 --> 00:39:46.300
So when we talk about apps--

00:39:46.300 --> 00:39:48.580
and this happened
more so in the past,

00:39:48.580 --> 00:39:51.070
because now, when
we say apps, people

00:39:51.070 --> 00:39:54.460
understand that even the backend
code, the backend systems

00:39:54.460 --> 00:39:56.540
of records are also apps.

00:39:56.540 --> 00:39:58.000
But when we talk
about app, we're

00:39:58.000 --> 00:39:59.470
just not talking
about mobile apps.

00:39:59.470 --> 00:40:00.844
It could be an
intel integration.

00:40:00.844 --> 00:40:03.640
It could be another business
unit within your enterprise

00:40:03.640 --> 00:40:06.100
that want to consume your APIs.

00:40:06.100 --> 00:40:09.820
Even though we call that an app
from a construct consumability

00:40:09.820 --> 00:40:13.330
standpoint, yeah, absolutely,
they're not always mobile apps.

00:40:13.330 --> 00:40:15.944
You can use Apigee even if
they're not mobile apps.

00:40:20.300 --> 00:40:25.754
The third question we have is,
do I have to rewrite my code--

00:40:25.754 --> 00:40:26.670
I think it's rewrite--

00:40:29.450 --> 00:40:33.640
to use Apigee's API
management features?

00:40:33.640 --> 00:40:34.490
No.

00:40:34.490 --> 00:40:35.930
So this is one
advantage of having

00:40:35.930 --> 00:40:37.430
Apigee as an abstraction layer.

00:40:40.040 --> 00:40:42.377
Once you put Apigee out there--

00:40:45.585 --> 00:40:47.710
at a very high level, we
talked about API services.

00:40:47.710 --> 00:40:53.090
That really is a proxy to
your backend implementation.

00:40:53.090 --> 00:40:55.974
So even if you have a
legacy backend that has,

00:40:55.974 --> 00:40:57.390
let's say, a poor
performance, you

00:40:57.390 --> 00:40:59.230
don't have to
completely rewrite that.

00:40:59.230 --> 00:41:00.240
You can if you want to.

00:41:00.240 --> 00:41:01.570
But with Apigee,
what you're doing is

00:41:01.570 --> 00:41:02.570
you're putting a facade.

00:41:02.570 --> 00:41:03.650
You're putting a proxy.

00:41:03.650 --> 00:41:05.608
And there, you are doing
things like mediation.

00:41:05.608 --> 00:41:08.040
So you don't have to
rewrite your backend

00:41:08.040 --> 00:41:09.257
to be able to do that.

00:41:13.984 --> 00:41:15.400
And then we have
another question.

00:41:15.400 --> 00:41:19.834
Can Apigee manage APIs
of our SaaS-based?

00:41:19.834 --> 00:41:21.250
I think this is a
common question.

00:41:21.250 --> 00:41:24.610
Like I say, backends, SaaS-based
backends, yeah, absolutely.

00:41:24.610 --> 00:41:27.160
You can do that.

00:41:27.160 --> 00:41:29.380
You can proxy your
SaaS applications

00:41:29.380 --> 00:41:31.600
and have Apigee proxy them.

00:41:31.600 --> 00:41:33.100
And there, you may
want to do things

00:41:33.100 --> 00:41:37.780
like integrate with your
existing identity provider,

00:41:37.780 --> 00:41:41.530
make it look more seamless,
translate the data formats.

00:41:41.530 --> 00:41:43.180
Or sometimes you're
seeing customers

00:41:43.180 --> 00:41:47.620
who take a SaaS-based
backend and on-prem-based

00:41:47.620 --> 00:41:49.454
and combine them and
create an API match-up.

00:41:49.454 --> 00:41:50.994
And that could be
a use case that you

00:41:50.994 --> 00:41:53.036
want to solve [INAUDIBLE]
development use cases

00:41:53.036 --> 00:41:55.360
for Apigee.

00:41:55.360 --> 00:41:57.860
The other question, I think
it's very specific to our Google

00:41:57.860 --> 00:41:59.860
Cloud managed offering.

00:41:59.860 --> 00:42:01.850
Can I leverage Google
Cloud Interconnect,

00:42:01.850 --> 00:42:04.580
even if I use Apigee
manged offering?

00:42:04.580 --> 00:42:06.614
Yeah, totally.

00:42:06.614 --> 00:42:09.030
That's something that we're
working through in partnership

00:42:09.030 --> 00:42:13.920
with the rest of the teams
within the Google Cloud.

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:16.760
You should be able to leverage
the Google Cloud Interconnect.

00:42:16.760 --> 00:42:21.020
The beauty of that is you will
have one seamless connectivity

00:42:21.020 --> 00:42:23.960
between your Apigee managed
offering, your cloud project,

00:42:23.960 --> 00:42:25.310
and your on-prem project.

00:42:25.310 --> 00:42:27.530
So the performance is awesome.

00:42:27.530 --> 00:42:30.770
I've seen customers
who testify to that.

00:42:30.770 --> 00:42:31.610
Absolutely.

00:42:31.610 --> 00:42:33.158
Awesome benefits of doing that.

00:42:37.464 --> 00:42:38.880
I think the other
question we have

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:40.254
